{
  "reasons": {
    "aliaspin": "A root alias does not steer a dependency's pick, as in npm and yarn (tests/berry.rs basic_installs_plain_scoped_aliased_fixed_and_ranged_dependencies); pnpm takes the alias's version.",
    "ancestor": "jpm resolves each version of a package once, wherever it is reached, so a pick cannot lean on the versions resolved higher up one path to it; pnpm prefers those. The project's own dependencies do steer picks, as in pnpm.",
    "dirbin": "directories.bin is not read: jpm records bins from the registry's manifest when it resolves, before any tarball is fetched (npm writes directories.bin out as bin when it packs).",
    "nocommon": "No version fits both consumers' peer ranges: jpm gives each its own (tests/cli.rs consumers_missing_one_peer_share_a_version_that_fits_them_all), and fails where one range has no version at all, as pnpm does for has-foo101-peer alone; pnpm installs neither.",
    "path": "A registry package that depends on a path (link: or file:) is refused: only the root and workspaces may (README, Directory dependencies). pnpm resolves the path inside the package.",
    "peerbin": "pnpm links the bins of a peer it installs into the root's node_modules/.bin, as if the root depended on it; jpm links the bins of the root's own dependencies only.",
    "peerparent": "The peers of a peer jpm installs come from the package that asked for it (its @pnpm/y 1.0.0); pnpm puts a peer it installs at the root, so its peers come from there (@pnpm/y 2.0.0).",
    "platform": "jpm refuses a required dependency that does not run on this platform (EBADPLATFORM), as npm does; pnpm installs it with a warning. tests/cli.rs skips_a_dev_dependency_for_another_platform pins this.",
    "range": "Among several aliases of a peer's package at one level, jpm takes the newest in the peer's range (peer-c 1.0.1 of 1.0.0, 1.0.1 and 2.0.0), as it does for plain versions; pnpm takes the newest alias, out of range too. A peer the scope has only out of range is linked, with a warning, as in pnpm.",
    "variant": "jpm keeps one copy of each version of a package, with one set of peers; pnpm makes another copy for each peer context along a path."
  },
